1. A method for estimating left and right eigenvector phases of a vibratory flow tube in a Coriolis flowmeter, the method comprising:

   driving the vibratory flow tube with a first driver and a second driver disposed at spaced-apart locations on the vibratory flow tube;

   applying to the first driver and the second driver respective drive signals that are out of phase with one another so as to excite an anti-symmetric vibration component of the vibratory flow tube;

   amplitude modulating the respective drive signals to produce excitation at a plurality of off-resonance excitation frequencies relative to a drive-mode resonant frequency of the vibratory flow tube;

   sensing motion of the vibratory flow tube with a first pickoff and a second pickoff disposed at spaced-apart locations on the vibratory flow tube;

   for each of the plurality of off-resonance excitation frequencies, determining a pickoff phase relationship from respective pickoff signals generated by the first pickoff and the second pickoff;

   determining, from the pickoff phase relationships at the plurality of off-resonance excitation frequencies, a frequency-dependent phase response of the vibratory flow tube;

   separating the frequency-dependent phase response into a left-side eigenvector phase estimate and a right-side eigenvector phase estimate associated with respective sides of the vibratory flow tube; and

   storing or using the left-side eigenvector phase estimate and the right-side eigenvector phase estimate to correct a measured Coriolis phase difference or to determine a mass flow rate through the Coriolis flowmeter.

A method of operating a vibratory flowmeter, the method comprising:

exciting a vibration mode of a flow tube by applying respective drive signals to first and second drivers coupled to the flow tube, the respective drive signals being amplitude-modulated and having carrier components that are substantially out of phase with one another so as to produce a selected modal excitation of the flow tube;

sweeping or otherwise applying the amplitude-modulated drive signals at a plurality of excitation frequencies offset from a resonant frequency of the vibration mode;

receiving, from one or more pickoff sensors coupled to the flow tube, pickoff signals representative of motion of the flow tube responsive to the excitation at the plurality of excitation frequencies;

determining, from phase relationships between the applied drive signals and the responsive pickoff signals at the plurality of off-resonance excitation frequencies, an estimate of a phase of a left eigenvector associated with the vibration mode;

measuring a pickoff phase of at least one of the pickoff signals during excitation of the vibration mode; and

determining, based at least in part on the measured pickoff phase and the estimated phase of the left eigenvector, a phase of a right eigenvector associated with the vibration mode of the flow tube.

1. **A vibratory flowmeter system**, comprising:  
   a flow tube (130, 130′) configured to convey a process fluid and to vibrate in a plurality of vibration modes;  
   first and second drivers (180L, 180R) coupled to the flow tube at spaced-apart locations and configured to impart vibratory motion to the flow tube;  
   first and second pickoffs (170L, 170R) coupled to the flow tube and configured to generate first and second pickoff signals representative of motion of the flow tube; and  
   meter electronics operatively coupled to the first and second drivers and the first and second pickoffs, the meter electronics being configured to generate **N+1 independent drive signals** for exciting the flow tube, determine an off-resonance modal phase response from the first and second pickoff signals, and infer a phase associated with at least one resonant vibration mode of the flow tube based on the off-resonance modal phase response.

2. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the meter electronics are configured to drive the first and second drivers with respective combinations of the N+1 independent drive signals to excite a plurality of modal components of the flow tube.

3. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the N+1 independent drive signals comprise a number of independent excitations greater than a number N of modal parameters to be estimated, thereby enabling modal separation or overdetermined estimation of a modal phase condition.

4. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the off-resonance modal phase response is determined at one or more drive frequencies offset from a resonant frequency of the flow tube.

5. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the meter electronics are further configured to estimate a **resonant eigenvector phase** corresponding to a resonant eigenvector of the flow tube using the inferred phase associated with the at least one resonant vibration mode.

6. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 5, wherein the resonant eigenvector phase estimation is used to compensate for modal coupling, asymmetry, damping, fluid loading, structural imbalance, or pickoff/driver placement effects.

7. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the first and second pickoff signals are processed to determine a relative phase between motion sensed at the first pickoff (170L) and motion sensed at the second pickoff (170R).

8. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 7, wherein the relative phase is used, together with the off-resonance modal phase inference, to determine a corrected flow-related phase or time delay.

9. The vibratory flowmeter system of claim 1, wherein the flow tube comprises a single flow tube (130) or a paired flow tube arrangement (130′).

10. **A method of operating a vibratory flowmeter**, comprising:  
    applying N+1 independent drive signals to first and second drivers (180L, 180R) coupled to a flow tube (130, 130′);  
    sensing motion of the flow tube using first and second pickoffs (170L, 170R);  
    generating first and second pickoff signals from the sensed motion;  
    determining, from the first and second pickoff signals, an off-resonance modal phase response of the flow tube;  
    inferring a resonant modal phase from the off-resonance modal phase response; and  
    estimating a resonant eigenvector phase of the flow tube based at least in part on the inferred resonant modal phase.

11. The method of claim 10, further comprising exciting the flow tube at a frequency displaced from a resonant frequency and extrapolating or mapping the measured off-resonance phase response to a phase condition at resonance.

12. The method of claim 10, further comprising determining a corrected flow measurement using the estimated resonant eigenvector phase.

13. The method of claim 10, wherein applying the N+1 independent drive signals comprises independently controlling amplitudes, phases, or frequencies of drive signals supplied to the first and second drivers.

14. The method of claim 10, wherein estimating the resonant eigenvector phase comprises resolving a modal phase ambiguity that is not directly measurable at resonance.

15. **Meter electronics for a vibratory flowmeter**, the meter electronics being configured to:  
    generate N+1 independent drive signals for first and second drivers (180L, 180R) coupled to a flow tube (130, 130′);  
    receive first and second pickoff signals from first and second pickoffs (170L, 170R);  
    compute an off-resonance modal phase response of the flow tube;  
    infer, from the off-resonance modal phase response, a resonant modal phase; and  
    estimate a resonant eigenvector phase for a resonant vibration mode of the flow tube.
